cartridge "COMET CATCHER/D.RUPERT/1986"

' COMET CATCHER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Cometcatcher" (The Rupert Report #28) by
' Dale Rupert, Ahoy! April 1986 (Commodore 128 BASIC 7.0, orig/). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The original is not a game but "a real-world simulation of astronomical
' proportions": a comet starts 3 AU left of the Sun and 1.2 AU up at
' 12.55 km/s, and every step (10 days) the listing works out the Sun's pull
' (G M / r^2 along the line to the Sun), updates the velocity and the
' position and plots a point of the orbit, printing the escape velocity,
' the speed, the distance in AU and the days gone by. Keys pause it,
' switch SLOW/FAST and make the time step 10% longer or shorter.
'
' Here the same physics runs as a game: comets come in from the edge of
' the field and swing round the Sun on real inverse-square orbits (the
' first one of every game is the listing's comet), with their tails always
' pointing away from the Sun. Fly the catcher into a comet to catch it:
' it is worth its speed in km/s times the wave number, so a comet caught
' fast near the Sun pays most. A comet that falls into the Sun or flies off
' the field is a miss, and so is flying the catcher into the Sun's corona;
' the fifth miss ends the game. Eight comets a wave; every wave the clock
' runs about 10% faster (the listing's "speed up" key), and from wave 3
' three comets can be in the field at once. Hold a button to slow time to
' half speed (the listing's "slow down" key) while the gauge lasts.
'
' From the original: the inverse-square pull, G M of the Sun, the first
' comet's start (-3, 1.2) AU and 12.55 km/s, the screen 4 AU high (here the
' 168-pixel field, 42 pixels an AU), the readouts R (AU) and V (km/s) of the
' comet nearest the catcher (the listing printed them for its one comet),
' a faster or slower clock.
'
' Liberties: the listing is a simulation with no goal; the catcher,
' scoring, misses, waves and the launch table (launch.py: 32 orbits checked
' with the game's own integer step, entries 0-3 the listing's comet and its
' mirror images) are new. The step is one day (the listing's 10, much too
' coarse for comets that pass close to the Sun) in fixed point: 1/256
' pixel positions, 1/4096 pixel a step velocities, the pull read from a
' table of G M / r^3 by r^2 bands (phys.py). The orbit trail the listing
' plotted is left out (comet tails instead). The escape velocity and day
' count readouts are dropped for room. A comet closer than 6 pixels
' (0.14 AU) falls into the Sun. The catcher flies straight (no gravity) so a
' human can steer it; its 14-pixel corona keeps it from camping at the Sun
' where every falling comet passes.
' The orbit step and an 8 x 16 multiply are in Z80 (CcStep, CcMul) so
' three comets at the fastest clock fit in one frame; a caught comet's
' points are added a frame or two later (ScoreGot) and the status line is
' redrawn on quiet frames, for the same reason. Points are whole km/s
' (x 330 / 256 of the 1/32-pixel speed). Waves add 26/256 to the clock
' up to 448/256 (wave 9). Diagonals move the catcher on both axes.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick flies the catcher, either
' button slows time. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

